## Why This Is Asked

This question tests multi-account Terraform patterns: provider aliases, cross-account data sources, and deterministic apply order.

## Key Concepts

- Aliased providers
- Cross-account data sources
- Data referencing across providers
- depends_on for ordering
- Credentials management via profiles and assume_role

## Code Example

```hcl
provider "aws" {
  alias   = "prod"
  region  = "us-east-1"
  profile = "prod"
}
provider "aws" {
  alias   = "audit"
  region  = "us-east-1"
  profile = "audit"
}

data "aws_caller_identity" "prod" {
  provider = aws.prod
}

data "aws_s3_bucket" "prod_bucket" {
  provider = aws.prod
  bucket   = "my-prod-bucket"
}

data "aws_iam_policy_document" "audit" {
  provider = aws.audit
  statement {
    actions   = ["s3:GetObject"]
    resources = ["${data.aws_s3_bucket.prod_bucket.arn}/*"]
    principals {
      type        = "AWS"
      identifiers = ["arn:aws:iam::${data.aws_caller_identity.prod.account_id}:root"]
    }
  }
}

resource "aws_s3_bucket" "audit_bucket" {
  provider = aws.audit
  bucket   = "audit-bucket"
}

resource "aws_iam_policy" "audit_policy" {
  provider = aws.audit
  name     = "CrossAccountGetObject"
  policy   = data.aws_iam_policy_document.audit.json
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you rotate credentials for the aliased providers safely?
- How would you test this cross-account permission in a CI workflow?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to implement policy-as-code governance for Terraform Cloud, ensuring prod safety beyond backend controls.

## Key Concepts

- Terraform Cloud and Sentinel governance
- Env-to-workspace mapping and state linkage
- CI integration with GitHub Actions for plan/apply gating
- Policy design distinguishing prod vs non-prod changes
- Local and CI testing of policies; post-merge audit of policy outcomes

## Code Example

```sentinel
import "tfplan/v2"

check = func(plan) {
  hasProdChange = false
  for _, rc in tfplan.resource_changes {
    if strings.contains(rc.address, "envs/prod/") {
      hasProdChange = true
      break
    }
  }
  // If prod resources are touched, deny unless approved in PR metadata
  if hasProdChange {
    return false
  }
  return true
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you evolve the policy to support time-bound approvals or multi-degree approvals?
- How would you validate policy effectiveness across 100s of PRs and detect gaps in coverage?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests understanding of multi-account setups and provider aliasing in modules. It also checks proper targeting and drift-free validation.

## Key Concepts
- Terraform providers and aliases
- Module-level provider configuration
- For_each environment handling
- Plan vs apply drift checks

## Code Example
```hcl
provider "aws" {
  alias = "dev"
  # credentials for dev account
}

provider "aws" {
  alias = "prod"
  # credentials for prod account
}

module "storage" {
  for_each = var.environments
  source   = "./modules/storage"
  providers = {
    aws = aws.dev
    aws.prod = aws.prod
  }
}
```
